{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"## Phase 1 RCA — confirmed root cause, with runtime proof\n\n**Confirmed: the drain lock genuinely fails to serialise.** Driven at runtime, not inferred\nfrom the path code. Two worktree views of ONE store (`.tickets-tracker` symlinked to the\ncanonical store, exactly as `make worktree` provisions):\n\n```\nwtA lock = .../wtA/.rebar/enrich-drain.lock   acquired = True\nwtB lock = .../wtB/.rebar/enrich-drain.lock   acquired = True\ndistinct lock paths      : True\nBOTH hold simultaneously : True\ncanonical .rebar         : []          <- nothing ever lands on the store\n```\n\nDiscriminating counter-experiment (same harness, `_rebar_dir` resolved through symlinks):\nsame single lock path, second acquire returns `None`, lock lands in the canonical `.rebar`.\nSo the mechanism is precisely the missing resolution, not something else keying the lock.\n\n### Root cause (one changeable artifact)\n\n`enrich_drain._rebar_dir` (`src/rebar/llm/enrich_drain.py:40-42`) derives every per-store\nsidecar as `os.path.dirname(tracker) + \"/.rebar\"` with no symlink resolution, so the drain\nlock (`:45-46`) and drain log (`:184-185`) are keyed on whatever path string the caller held\nrather than on the store. `overlap/queue._gate_marker_path` (`queue.py:257-259`) repeats the\nomission for `enrich-gate.json`.\n\n### What the lock is actually protecting — per-STORE, unambiguously\n\nEvery resource `drain()` touches is store-scoped: `_stale_digest_ids` and\n`pending_enrichment` do `os.listdir(tracker)` on the shared store; `enqueue`/`claim`/\n`mark_done` append events into the shared tracker; `enrich`+`ds.emit` write digest sidecars\nin the shared store. There is **no per-worktree resource in `drain()` at all** — so the\nticket's premise holds and worktree-locality is an accident of string derivation, not a\ndesign choice. The `_acquire_advisory_lock` ACCEPTED-RACE comment\n(`enrich_drain.py:146-149`) reasons explicitly at store level: \"the correctness boundary is\nthe per-ticket optimistic queue claim … which a second drainer loses.\"\n\n### The ticket's premise, refined by evidence\n\nThe **write path was already correct**. `event_append.py:342,357` and `push.py:484,502` both\ncanonicalise before calling `maybe_drain`, so ordinary ticket writes from any worktree\nalready contend on the one canonical lock (confirmed live: the running drain's `argv[1]` is\nthe canonical tracker). The live leak is the **`rebar enrich` CLI**\n(`_cli/__init__.py:610` -> `config.tracker_dir()`, unresolved):\n\n```\nCLI   lock path : /Users/joeoakhart/fix-heron-drainlock/.rebar/enrich-drain.lock\nWRITE lock path : /Users/joeoakhart/rebar/.rebar/enrich-drain.lock\nSAME lock for one store? : False\n```\n\nTwo entry points to the same store, from the same worktree, in the same process, taking two\ndifferent locks. Corroborated on this host: a leaked worktree lock at\n`/Users/joeoakhart/rp05-cli-registry/.rebar/enrich-drain.lock` (dead pid 25770, 26h old) and\nstray `enrich-drain.log` files in two further worktrees. That scattering is also why 3198 saw\nonly four tracebacks in the canonical log — the rest went to ~100 worktree log files.\n\nThe deeper defect is that the module enforces its own \"one store, one drain lock\" invariant\n**nowhere in the module**: it is entirely dependent on every caller canonicalising, and one\ncaller does not.\n\n### Legitimacy: `defect`, against three cited authorities\n\n1. `_store/lock.py:150-153` — `canonical_tracker`: *\"Resolve to a canonical path … so\n   symlinked and real-path callers contend on the SAME lock file.\"* The project's stated\n   canonicalisation contract for lock files.\n2. `_store/hlc.py:80-83` — the neighbouring `.rebar/hlc.lock` already resolves internally.\n3. `_proc.detached_child_cwd` (`_proc.py:112-116`, landed by bug 3198) — realpath is\n   *\"load-bearing rather than cosmetic — a provisioned worktree's `.tickets-tracker` is a\n   SYMLINK into the main checkout.\"*\n\nAnd `doctor_locks.scan_locks` (`:328-329`) already canonicalises before reporting the drain\nlock, so `rebar doctor` has always described the canonical lock while the drain took a\nworktree one — doctor is structurally unable to see the lock that wedges that worktree. The\nfix makes the drain match the contract doctor already reports.\n\n### Contention analysis — net contention goes DOWN, no deadlock\n\n* **Non-blocking.** `O_CREAT|O_EXCL`, a 2-iteration loop, no wait/sleep/retry-until-acquired.\n  On `None`, `drain()` logs one WARNING and returns `{\"skipped\": \"lock-held\"}`. `maybe_drain`\n  never raises; both write-path callers additionally swallow.\n* **Deadlock impossible.** No path takes the store write lock and then waits on the advisory\n  lock — the advisory acquire never waits at all.\n* **No caller assumed it was cheap because it was per-process.** `maybe_drain`'s cheapness is\n  the O(1) gate, which returns before the lock is touched.\n* **Contention decreases.** Today up to N worktree drainers can run concurrently on one\n  store, each doing full store scans and each contending on the *store write lock* for\n  CLAIM/DONE appends — the 2.8-3.6 s profile `queue.py:220-222` measured. Store-scoping\n  reduces that to one drainer; the rest pay one `os.open` and one log line.\n* **Blast-radius amplification: checked and refuted.** The concern was that a leaked\n  canonical lock would disable enrichment everywhere instead of in one worktree. Measured\n  against the live stamp shape (`ns=- start=-`, a dead pid on this host):\n  `stamped_file_is_stale` returns **True immediately** — even fresh, because a dead pid on\n  the same host is positive proof, so no wall-clock wait is needed. Verified against the real\n  leaked lock on this host as well. A leaked lock is reclaimed by the very next drain.\n\n### Fix: restoration, not creation\n\nApplying an existing, named, already-shipped helper (`canonical_tracker`) at the one place\nthat skipped it. No new lock, no new protocol, no new failure mode for callers, no new\ncapability, no new dependency. The coordination was documented as store-scoped in three\nshipped artifacts before this bug; the drain simply never met the contract.\n\n### Defect origin — `caused_by` leggy-heedless-mosquito (c1de-d6a0-6cef-4135)\n\nIntroducing commit `9ad46965bd` (2026-07-09), *\"feat(overlap): Tier-1 opportunistic detached\ndrain + rebar enrich CLI\"*, `rebar-ticket: c1de-d6a0-6cef-4135`. `git log -L 40,43` on\n`enrich_drain.py` names that single commit — the helper has never resolved symlinks.\n\n**Date test applied.** The obligation predates the defect: `canonical_tracker` landed\n2026-06-12 in `57114212cf`, four weeks before the drain. So this is not the\nblame-the-consumer trap — the drain was authored *after* the contract existed and did not\nmeet it, and the same commit canonicalised at two of its three entry points while missing the\nthird and putting no resolution in the module's own derivation. The fix site is the cause\nsite here, and the date test confirms rather than contradicts that: the implicated lines were\nborn wrong in that commit.\n\n**Class: `preexisting`** — never worked; not a regression."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1787303042310392001,"uuid":"aac53e29-da37-4770-9c82-e5339ac6c9a3"}